## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of closures and promises in a realistic data loader, including deduplicating in-flight requests and time-based invalidation.

## Key Concepts
- Closures and encapsulation
- Promise deduplication for identical requests
- Mutable in-memory cache with TTL
- Edge cases: error handling, cache busting

## Code Example
```javascript
function makeCachedFetcher(fetchFn) {
  const TTL = 2 * 60 * 1000;
  const cache = new Map();
  return async function load(id) {
    const entry = cache.get(id);
    const now = Date.now();
    if (entry?.value !== undefined && (now - entry.time) < TTL) {
      return entry.value;
    }
    if (entry?.promise) {
      return entry.promise;
    }
    const p = fetchFn(id).then(res => {
      cache.set(id, { value: res, time: Date.now() });
      return res;
    }).catch(err => {
      cache.delete(id);
      throw err;
    });
    cache.set(id, { promise: p });
    return p;
  };
}

// Usage
const fakeFetch = async (id) => ({ id, value: 'val-'+id });
const loadUser = makeCachedFetcher(fakeFetch);
loadUser(1).then(console.log); // { id: 1, value: 'val-1' }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you expose a manual invalidate(id) API and a global clearCache() for testing? 
- How would you adapt this pattern to avoid memory leaks in long-lived apps?

Closures enable functional patterns: private state, currying, and memoization. Trade-offs: closures keep references alive, increasing memory footprint; avoid capturing huge objects. Edge cases: loops with var can close over the wrong index; use let. In UIs, closures can lead to stale state if components re-render without updating bindings; debouncing/memoization illustrate how closures bind behavior to a specific creation context. Debounce example:
```
const debounce = (fn, wait) => {
  let t;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), wait);
  };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates per-tenant cache with TTL, concurrency control, and a small, framework-agnostic loader.

## Key Concepts

- Closures and per-tenant caches
- Promise deduplication for in-flight fetches
- TTL eviction and 50-entry cache cap
- Framework-agnostic API suitable for progressive enhancement

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch
function TenantPageLoader() {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  const ttl = 2 * 60 * 1000;
  const max = 50;

  function key(t,p){return t+':'+p}
  async function fetchPage(t, p){
    const url = `/api/${t}/items?page=${p}`;
    const resp = await fetch(url);
    const data = await resp.json();
    cache.set(key(t,p), { data, expiresAt: Date.now()+ttl });
    inFlight.delete(key(t,p));
    return data;
  }
  return {
    getPage: function(tenantId, page){
      const k = key(tenantId, page);
      const now = Date.now();
      if (cache.has(k)){
        const entry = cache.get(k);
        if (entry.expiresAt>now) return Promise.resolve(entry.data);
        cache.delete(k);
      }
      if (inFlight.has(k)) return inFlight.get(k);
      const p = fetchPage(tenantId, page);
      inFlight.set(k, p);
      p.then(()=>{},()=>{});
      // simple eviction
      if (cache.size>max){
        const first = cache.keys().next().value; cache.delete(first);
      }
      return p;
    },
    clearCache: function(){ cache.clear(); inFlight.clear(); }
  }
}
```

